  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{\u2032}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(1)=3,$ and since ${f}^{\u2032}(x)={x}^{2}(5x+1),$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(1)=(1)^{2}(5\\cdot 1+1)=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=3+6(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1.3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.3) & \u2248L(1.3) \\\\  & =3+6(1.3-1) \\\\  & =3+6(0.3) \\\\  & =3+1.8 \\\\  & =4.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
